{"records":[{"id":"w9bp8436r9","title":"Detection and quantification of Amatoxin in wild mushrooms from North-East India using HPLC-PDA method for food safety purposes","description":"Misidentification and ingestion of poisonous mushrooms pose significant threats to food safety, particularly in Mizoram, India, where over ten fatalities due to mushroom poisoning have been reported in the past decade (2013-2023). This study aimed to address this critical issue by identifying and quantifying the cause of death due to consumption of wild mushroom from Champhai district, Mizoram, India and to test the reliability of HPLC-PDA for detection and quantification of amatoxins. HPLC-PDA confirmed the presence of α-amanitin in Amanita virosa and Amanita bisporigera in the samples. α-amanitin is a water-soluble, heat-stable, and highly toxic cyclic octapeptide present in the genus Amanita, which includes Amanita phalloides, Amanita verna, and Amanita virosa. Amanitin cytotoxicity arises from the inhibition of RNA polymerases, namely RNA polymerase II, which obstructs mRNA production in kidney and liver cells. Validation of the method demonstrated good precision and accuracy, with LOD and LOQ values of 88 ng g-1 and 210 ng g-1, respectively. The method was successfully applied to quantify α-amanitin in ten wild mushroom samples, revealing its presence only in Amanita virosa (1.17 mg g-1) and Amanita bisporigera (1.91 mg g-1) species. These findings underscore the importance of accurate α-amanitin detection methods in ensuring food safety and public health, particularly in regions prone to mushroom poisoning incidents. It is noteworthy that this study marks the initial exploration for detection and quantification of α-amanitin from poisonous mushrooms found in the wild regions of Champhai district in Mizoram, representing the first report of such in the area.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-18T15:56:24.379Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/w9bp8436r9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/w9bp8436r9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"David malsawmtluanga","last_name":"Chhakchhuak","orcid":null},{"first_name":"J","last_name":"Lalbiaknunga","orcid":"0000-0003-3914-8726"},{"first_name":"Laldinkima","last_name":"Chhangte","orcid":"0000-0001-5641-1480"},{"first_name":"Lal","last_name":"awmpuia","orcid":"0009-0009-7397-001X"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Toxicology","Mycology","High Performance Liquid Chromatography","Mycotoxin"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"khb3vf66gj","title":"Subcellular Localization Shapes the Fate of RNA Polymerase III","description":"RNA Pol III plays a vital role in transcription and as a viral-DNA sensor, but how it is assembled and distributed within cells remain poorly understood. Here, we show that Pol III is assembled with chaperones in the cytoplasm and forms transcription-dependent protein clusters upon transport into the nucleus. The largest subunit (RPC1) depletion through an auxin-inducible degron leads to rapid degradation and disassembly of Pol III complex in the nucleus and cytoplasm, respectively. This generates a pool of partially assembled Pol III intermediates, which can be rapidly mobilized into the nucleus upon the restoration of RPC1. Our study highlights the critical role of subcellular localization in determining Pol III's fate and provide insight into the dynamic regulation of nuclear Pol III levels and the origin of cytoplasmic Pol III complexes involved in mediating viral immunity.\n\nIn this file, the result of Western blot and Cell Imaging was listed as Figures shown. A brief message of this folder was characterized as below:\nThe mES cells were treated with Doxycycline, auxin, Leptomycin B (LMB), MG132, Celastrol (an HSP90 inhibitor), VER-155008 (an HSP70 inhibitor), α-amanitin (a Pol II inhibitor), ML60218 (a Pol III inhibitor), and Actinomycin D (a pan-transcription inhibitor) at the common used concentration and treatment time. The details can be found in the methods section of our paper.\nThe Raw image was captured using Andor's Dragonfly microscope with .ims type, and it could be opened by imaris (oxford instrument) or ImageJ (FiJi).\n\nIn the folder, some abbreviation were indicated as： \nTC: time course; CN: cytoplasm and nuclear fraction; LMinh: LMB and/or MG132 inhibitor treatment; BIOT: halotag biotin ligand","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-18T14:42:03.487Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/khb3vf66gj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/khb3vf66gj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kai","last_name":"Tian","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Protein Degradation","RNA Polymerase","Auxin","Cell Fractionation","Cellular Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"548vj9sv6p","title":"MushroomNet V1: A Multi-Class Image Dataset for Accurate Mushroom Species Identification","description":"This sample was taken in the National Mushroom Development and Extension Center, Savar, Dhaka, Bangladesh. The total number of raw images of various species of mushrooms obtained in the mushroom growing environment under natural lighting was 743. The pictures were taken directly of the actual mushroom production setups in order to make sure that they would vary in terms of texture, shape and lighting conditions.\n\nThe data is divided into eight mushroom types as in:\n\n1. Ear Mushroom: 72\n\n2. Golden Oyster Mushroom: 57\n\n3. Gray Oyster Mushroom: 44\n\n4. Lion’s Mane Mushroom: 15\n\n5. Milky Mushroom: 27\n\n6. Oyster (Po10) Mushroom: 230\n\n7. Pink Oyster Mushroom: 225\n\n8. Reishi Mushroom: 73\n\nThe dataset offers practical mushrooms images which can be used in agricultural technology research and development and intelligent farming systems.\n\n\nGeographical Location\n\nThe entire mushroom images have been gathered at the National Mushroom Development and Extension center in Savar, Dhaka, Bangladesh. This is a renowned government-funded facility that focuses on mushroom research and mushroom cultivation and training producing a range of mushroom species that are grown under both controlled and natural environments.\n\nAddress: Savar, Dhaka, Bangladesh.\n\nLatitude: 23.85071° N\n\nLongitude: 90.25792° E\n\n\nUse of the Dataset\n\n1. To create and test machine learning and deep learning models in classifying mushroom species.\n\n2. To assist in computer vision research in agriculture.\n\n3. To develop automated systems of mushroom identification based on the image technique.\n\n4. To help researchers to create AI-based solutions in smart farming.\n\n5. To give a benchmark data set on mushroom image classification tasks.\n\n6. To assist in the creation of the mobile or web-based applications related to the mushroom recognition.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-05T14:04:28.754Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/548vj9sv6p","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/548vj9sv6p"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Md Parvez","last_name":"Kabir","orcid":"0009-0007-9871-4317"},{"first_name":"Anukul","last_name":"Chandra","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Computer Vision","Image Processing","Image Classification","Deep Learning","Agriculture"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"js7njmkcc2","title":"Effectiveness of Baccaurea ramiflora Leaf Extract as a Green Corrosion Inhibitor for Carbon Steel in Hydrochloric Acid Solution","description":"Effectiveness of Baccaurea ramiflora Leaf Extract as a Green Corrosion Inhibitor for Carbon Steel in Hydrochloric Acid Solution","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-02T15:29:46.224Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/js7njmkcc2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/js7njmkcc2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Banumathi","last_name":"R","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Arun","last_name":"Thirumurugan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Corrosion Inhibitor"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jtnc89dbn5","title":"Hemin-Binding Site Competition and Hemin Cytotoxicity Evaluation: Extension of \"Discovery of a novel miconazole analogue as a dual apo-IDO1 inhibitor and ABCB1 modulator for cancer immunotherapy and chemosensitizing\"","description":"This repository contains a graphical figure for a functional Hemin-Binding Site Competition Assay and an LDH Cytotoxicity Assay. The functional assay assesses how the addition of exogenous hemin modulates the suppression of kynurenine (Kyn) production by our Apo-IDO1 inhibitor (i.e., 1g), confirming a competitive mechanism of action. The LDH cytotoxicity assay serves as an experimental control, demonstrating that the concentrations of hemin used in the competition study do not compromise cell viability. These experiments provide further validation of the molecular hypothesis reported in our paper in the European Journal of Pharmaceutical Sciences (DOI: 10.1016/j.ejps.2026.107605).","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-11-05T09:07:23.673Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jtnc89dbn5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jtnc89dbn5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yi-Hsien","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yun-Yun","last_name":"Ho","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Suat","last_name":"Sari","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jóhannes","last_name":"Reynisson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Euphemia","last_name":"Leung","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chun Hei Antonio","last_name":"Cheung","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/04kwvgz42","name":"Hacettepe University","grant_id":"TSA-2023-20713"},{"id":"https://ror.org/02kv4zf79","name":"National Science and Technology Council","grant_id":"114-2320-B-006-025"}],"categories":["Immunology","Pharmacology","Cancer","Anticancer Drug"],"journal_issn":["09280987"]},{"id":"vkz52nv6nv","title":"BROAD TRANSCRIPTIONAL FIRING REPRESSES  BACTERICIDAL ACTIVITY IN HUMAN AIRWAY NEUTROPHILS","description":"Neutrophils are often considered terminally differentiated and poised for bacterial killing. In chronic diseases such as cystic fibrosis (CF), an unexplained paradox pits massive neutrophil presence against prolonged bacteria infections. Here, we show that neutrophils recruited to CF airways in vivo and in an in vitro transmigration model display rapid and broad transcriptional firing, leading to an upregulation of survival and anabolic genes, and a downregulation of antimicrobial genes. Newly transcribed RNAs are mirrored by the appearance of corresponding proteins, confirming active translation in these cells. Remarkably, treatment by the RNA polymerase II and III inhibitor -amanitin restored expression of key antimicrobial genes, and increased bactericidal capacity of CF airway neutrophils in vitro and in short-term sputum cultures ex vivo. Broadly, our findings show that neutrophil plasticity is regulated at the terminal organ via RNA and protein synthesis, leading to adaptations that profoundly impact their canonical functions (i.e., bacterial clearance).","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-09T11:32:49.753Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vkz52nv6nv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vkz52nv6nv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Diego","last_name":"Moncada","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rabindra","last_name":"Tirouvanziam","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Camilla","last_name":"Margaroli","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Proteomics","Applied Immunology","Cystic Fibrosis","Microarray","Transcriptomics","Neutrophil Response"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y68by8rz8h","title":"Dataset for potential application of CPC material synthesized from Rarak extract-copper ","description":"Copper-phytochemical coordination (CPC) materials synthesize from solvothermal reaction of phytochemicals in Rarak extract and copper ions. The CPC materials is investigated for their potential application including cytotoxicity assay against NIH-3T3 cell line, cytotoxicity assay toward seed germination on Arabidopsis thaliana plant model, and adsorption performance against various hazardous substances (dyes and heavy metals). This dataset contains the experimented value on the cytotoxicity assays and adsorption study of the CPC materials.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-17T10:07:00.327Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y68by8rz8h","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y68by8rz8h"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Nikko","last_name":"Setiawan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kuan-Chen","last_name":"Cheng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hui-Wen","last_name":"Lin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Adam","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Artik","last_name":"Angkawijaya","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hsien-Yi","last_name":"Hsu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shin-Ping","last_name":"Lin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chang-Wei","last_name":"Hsieh","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shella","last_name":"Santoso","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jenni","last_name":"Lie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jheng-Jhe","last_name":"Lu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Astrid","last_name":"Rahmawati","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Osamu","last_name":"Shimomura","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Suryadi","last_name":"Ismadji","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cytotoxicity","Adsorption","Biomolecular Material","Biomass"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wtrhpzyh5m","title":"A study of Identification of XAF1 as an endogenous AKT inhibitor. Chen et al","description":"Original western blot images of manuscript entitled “Identification of XAF1 as an endogenous AKT inhibitor” (CELL-REPORTS-D-22-04698R2) as a Research Article at Cell Reports.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-29T16:41:55.609Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wtrhpzyh5m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wtrhpzyh5m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"kangjunjie","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fyrcnvnp5y","title":"Supplementary Material-Safety and clinical response of TNF-α inhibitor adalimumab in the treatment of severe acne: A real-world study","description":"Supplementary Material for the paper \"Safety and clinical response of TNF-α inhibitor adalimumab in the treatment of severe acne: A real-world study\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-19T14:20:38.355Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fyrcnvnp5y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fyrcnvnp5y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhuokun","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yufei","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jialin","last_name":"Lian","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Danyang","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jiayi","last_name":"Han","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ruilian","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chenyue","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaoli","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Dermatology","Acne"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7wn4zc9gj9","title":"ZSP1601,a pan-PDE inhibitor for MASH Raw data","description":"This study aims to investigate the therapeutic effects and mechanism of action of ZSP1601, a novel non-specific phosphodiesterase small molecule inhibitor, in improving metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis (MASH). ZSP1601 was developed through structural modification and optimization based on the chemical framework of Pentoxifylline. It inhibits PDE activity, thereby elevating cyclic adenosine monophosphate levels and suppressing the transcription of tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α) gene, which may confer beneficial effects in the treatment of MASH. The research confirms the efficacy and mechanistic basis of ZSP1601 as a non-specific PDE small molecule inhibitor against MASH. The compound was identified as a non-specific PDE inhibitor through integrated approaches including chemical structure design, synthesis, molecular docking simulations, and in vitro enzymatic assays. Subsequent in vitro and in vivo pharmacodynamic models were employed to evaluate the therapeutic potential of ZSP1601 in MASH. Furthermore, pharmacokinetic studies demonstrated that ZSP1601 exhibits favorable pharmacokinetic properties suitable for oral administration. By combining medicinal chemistry design, chemical synthesis, pharmacological evaluation models, and pharmacokinetic analyses, this study elucidates the underlying mechanisms of ZSP1601 in ameliorating MASH and assesses its druggability. ZSP1601 significantly improved the NAS score and reduced liver fibrosis in MASH model animals by lowering TNF-α levels, exhibiting superior efficacy compared to the positive control compound Pentoxifylline. The development of ZSP1601 offers a novel therapeutic strategy for the treatment of MASH. Given its potential, ZSP1601 is currently being studied in phase II clinical trials.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-10T14:46:17.214Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7wn4zc9gj9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7wn4zc9gj9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"miao","last_name":"liu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Drug Discovery"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":130645,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":146,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":130645,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":130099,"restricted":64,"embargoed":482},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2174},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1399},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":593},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":451},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":440},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":414},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":409},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":361},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":295},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":256}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":128036},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1899},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":181},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":131},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":73},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":64},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":55},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":41},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":30},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":909},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":824},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":436},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":405},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":362}]}}